  "budgetDerivation": "# Prospective enthalpy budget for the closed-graph pilot\n\nThis is a mathematical/design specification, not a claim that an unimplemented\nintegrator has passed. Its implementation and source hash must be sealed before\nany trajectory. No running input or sealed reference is modified here.\n\nLet uRound=2^-53 be Float64 unit roundoff and gamma_k=k*uRound/(1-k*uRound).\nAssume finite normal arithmetic with no overflow or underflow in the budgeted\ntemperature operations. Refuse a violation of those assumptions, or explicitly\nadd a separately derived underflow term before sealing implementation.\n\nAt each step compute each retained heat-bond amount F once, add that same\nrepresented number to one endpoint and its negative to the other. Each node\nhas at most six incident bonds for the210 plane. Let H_i be its accumulated\nFloat64 heat increment. Update\n\n    d_i=fl(psiNew_i-psiOld_i)\n    TNew_i=fl(fl(TOld_i+H_i)+d_i).\n\nIn real arithmetic on the represented F numbers, their total is exactly zero.\nThe following conservative one-step bound covers heat accumulation, the two\ntemperature additions, and the rounded latent increment:\n\n    Bstep=delta*{\n       gamma_6 * 2*sum_bonds |F|\n       + gamma_2*sum_i (|TOld_i|+|H_i|+|d_i|)\n       + uRound*sum_i (|psiOld_i|+|psiNew_i|)\n    }.\n\nUse the actual maximum incident-bond count if the implementation changes the\ntopology; do not silently retain6 for another normal. The computation of F\ndoes not itself inject global heat because its rounded value is transferred\nwith both signs. Errors in F still affect evolution accuracy and belong in\ntimestep/operator controls, not an enthalpy repair.\n\nAccumulate nonnegative bound terms conservatively: an ordinary positive\nreduction of M terms is inflated by1/(1-gamma_(M-1)); similarly bound the\npositive per-step total accumulation or use outward-rounded/exact arithmetic.\nDo not compare against a downward-rounded estimate of Bstep. This is a\ncode-derived arithmetic envelope, not an empirically selected drift band.\n\nAt each retained probe evaluate the exact energy of the represented fields:\n\n    Eexact=Fraction(delta)*sum_i[Fraction(T_i)-Fraction(psi_i)-Fraction(b)],\n\nwhere Fraction converts each stored binary Float64 exactly and b is a fixed\nrecorded background. Integer/superaccumulator arithmetic can replace Fraction\nif verified against it. The difference Eexact(t)-Eexact(0) is then exact as a\nrational number before reporting/serialization. The closed-graph predicate is\n\n    |Eexact(t)-Eexact(0)| <= conservativeSum(Bstep through t).\n\nThis avoids conflating measurement subtraction/cancellation with physical\nnonconservation. Compensated sums remain useful descriptive crosschecks.\nNo temperature projection, uniform rescaling or post-step energy correction\nis allowed. A failed envelope is an implementation/numerical evidence failure;\nit is not repaired by changing the perturbation or interpreting a velocity.\n\nFor a later storage-Float32 arm, the signed extra source is explicitly\n\n    Rstep=delta*sum_i[(T32_i-T64_i)-(psi32_i-psi64_i)].\n\nEvaluate it with a declared accurate/exact signed accumulator and include its\nrounding envelope if needed. Test E32(t)-E32(0)-sum(Rstep) against the remaining\nFloat64 arithmetic bound. This describes storage casts only, not GPU fused\narithmetic. Initial casts also receive a separate measured Delta E.\n\nZero-integral initial pulses have their own discrete construction/rounding\nledger. Do not pretend an analytical zero integral guarantees zero after\nsampling, adding to a stored background or a Float32 cast. No initial residual\nis removed after seeing a trajectory.\n",
